3. Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W-2 job) — they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 due to TCJA suspension.
- If these are business expenses related to your web design clients (self-employment), they belong on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses and are deductible against your self-employment income. If so, include them on Schedule C.

4. Home office (100 sq ft exclusive use, rented dwelling)
- You qualify for the home-office deduction only for business use related to your self-employment (web design). You cannot claim a home-office deduction for W-2 employment (except very limited categories that rarely apply).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction. Easy, no Form 8829.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a portion of rent and allowable utilities/insurance to the business. Your rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Rent portion = 0.1449 × $28,800 ≈ $4,174. You can also pro-rate utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable shared home expenses. (As a renter you cannot claim depreciation for the dwelling.)
- The home-office deduction (either method) must be limited to business income after other business expenses. It reduces net profit on Schedule C and flows to both income tax and self-employment tax calculations.
- If you use the actual-expense method, file Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) with your return. With simplified, just report it on Schedule C.

Other notes about self-employment income
- Report web design income and business expenses on Schedule C (Form 1040).
- Compute self-employment tax on Schedule SE — roughly 15.3% of net self-employment income (subject to Social Security wage base limits). You may deduct half of the self-employment tax as an adjustment to income.
