Thanks — I can walk you through what is (generally) deductible, whether itemizing likely helps, how to treat the home office, which forms you’ll need, and an estimate of how the home-office method affects your tax picture. A few quick clarifying questions are below so I can make the numbers exact.

Assumptions I’ll use below unless you tell me otherwise
- The $8,900 is self‑employment income (you’re a sole proprietor doing web design) and the $780 are business expenses related to that work. If the $780 are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, those aren’t deductible (see note below).
- The $4,400 is student‑loan interest you actually paid in 2023 (not the loan origination years).
- You have no other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, big state income/property taxes, large medical expenses, etc.) unless you tell me otherwise.

What you can deduct (overview)
- Salary/W‑2: taxable wages = $82,350 (no deduction except itemized/standard).
- Self‑employment income (web design): report gross and deduct ordinary business expenses on Schedule C (Schedule C forms your net business profit/loss).
  - You can deduct the $780 as business expense on Schedule C (if they are business expenses).
  - You can deduct a home‑office share of your rent/utilities (see below) either using the simplified method or actual expenses via Form 8829.
- Interest income (Citi savings $1,560 and E*TRADE $195): taxable. Because total interest/dividends > $1,500, you’ll need Schedule B.
- Student‑loan interest: up to $2,500 deductible as an above‑the‑line deduction (on Schedule 1), phased out for MFJ at higher MAGI (you are well below the phaseout based on the numbers below).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay SE tax on net self‑employment income; half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Charitable donation: $2,000 cash gift to a qualified charity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) but only matters if you itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 if they are employee (W‑2) unreimbursed expenses. If the $780 are self‑employment expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Home‑office: simplified vs actual method (and what I recommend)
- You said the office is 100 sq ft exclusive use; house size 690 sq ft → business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
- Actual expense method: you allocate a share of actual home expenses to the business. With rent $2,400/month → annual rent = $28,800. Business portion = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,175.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., a portion of those can increase the deduction (but you didn’t list amounts).
  - To claim actual home‑office expenses you should file Form 8829 with your return (it computes the allowed deduction and any carryover).
- Important limitation: home‑office deduction for a sole proprietor cannot exceed net profit from that business. If net profit is smaller than the home‑office deduction, the excess may be suspended and carried forward.
- Recommendation: based on the rent you reported, the actual‑expense method gives a much larger deduction (≈$4,175) and will usually be better than the simplified $500 — document square footage, rent receipts, and any utilities you allocate.

Rough tax-impact comparison (illustrative)
(Using the assumptions above: W‑2 $82,350; interest $1,560; E*TRADE $195; self‑employment gross $8,900; business expenses $780; student loan interest paid in 2023 = $4,400 but deductible amount capped at $2,500.)

Option A — Actual home‑office allocation (≈$4,175)
- Net self‑employment income = 8,900 − 780 − 4,175 = 3,945
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% of net) ≈ $558 → half of that ≈ $279 (above‑the‑line deduction)
- Total income after net business = wages + interest + net SE = 82,350 + 1,560 + 195 + 3,945 = $88,050
- Subtract student loan interest deduction (max) $2,500 and half SE tax $279 → AGI ≈ $85,271
- Standard deduction for MFJ (2023) = $27,700 → estimated taxable income ≈ $57,571

Option B — Simplified home‑office ($500)
- Net self‑employment income = 8,900 − 780 − 500 = 7,620
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $1,077 → half ≈ $538 (above‑the‑line)
- Total income after net business = 82,350 + 1,560 + 195 + 7,620 = $91,725
- Subtract student loan interest $2,500 and half SE tax $538 → AGI ≈ $88,687
- Standard deduction $27,700 → estimated taxable income ≈ $60,987

Takeaway from these estimates: the actual‑expense method (if properly documented and allowed) produces a materially lower taxable income in your situation. (Numbers above are illustrative — I rounded and did not include possible QBI or state tax considerations.)

Other credits/deductions to note
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income. With your small Schedule C profit, you should be able to claim some QBI deduction; that will further reduce taxable income. QBI is claimed on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A depending on circumstances).
- Charitable $2,000: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction for MFJ is $27,700, that single $2,000 donation alone will usually not make itemizing worthwhile unless you have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/property taxes, big medical expenses, etc.).

Required forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student‑loan interest deduction and the deduction for half of SE tax are reported here
- Schedule B — for interest/dividends because interest + dividends > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $8,900 and business expenses ($780) and the home‑office deduction if using simplified or actual (if simplified, you report the amount on Schedule C; if actual you’ll use Form 8829)
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual‑expense method)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — QBI deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (e.g., if you discover additional itemizable deductions)

Records you should gather (and keep)
- 1099s (1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC) from clients (or records of income if no 1099s)
- 1099‑INT (Citi/E*TRADE) and any brokerage statements
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) or loan statements showing interest paid in 2023
- Rent receipts and proof of payment; utility amounts if you claim them as part of actual home expenses
- Documentation for the home‑office: floor plan/photo showing exclusive space, square footage calculation and overall home square footage
- Receipts for charitable donation and any other deductions
- Records of your $780 business expenses

A couple important caveats
- If the $780 are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses (not self‑employment), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law. If they were business expenses for your web design (self‑employment), they belong on Schedule C.
- Student‑loan interest is deductible only for interest paid in the tax year and if you meet other requirements (not claimed as a dependent, loans are qualified education loans, MAGI limits). If the $4,400 was paid in a prior year, it isn’t deductible for 2023.
- This is general information — consider using tax software or consulting a tax professional to prepare your return and confirm amounts, especially because a home‑office deduction and QBI interact with multiple forms and limits.

Questions for you so I can refine everything
1. Did you actually pay $4,400 of student‑loan interest during 2023 (and will you get Form 1098‑E or statements showing 2023 interest paid)?
2. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your self‑employment (web design) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
3. Do you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, state income tax paid, large medical expenses, IRA or HSA contributions, childcare, etc.)?
4. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from the two web‑design clients (or did you receive only cash/checks with no 1099)?

If you confirm the items above I can (a) produce a more exact AGI/taxable income estimate including an approximate federal income tax and self‑employment tax amount and (b) give step‑by‑step instructions for filling the needed forms (including exact lines to enter amounts).

