Short answer first
- For federal 2023, your married-filing-joint standard deduction is $27,700. Based on the items you listed (only $2,000 in cash charity and no mortgage interest or large SALT/property tax amounts given), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.
- Your web-design income (self-employment) is reported on Schedule C and you can claim a home-office deduction for the 100 sq ft exclusive office. Using the actual method (allocate a share of rent/utilities) will very likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest is potentially deductible (up to $2,500, subject to AGI phaseouts). Charitable contributions are deductible only if you itemize (so the $2,000 would not help if you take the standard deduction).
- Forms you will generally need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (business income/expenses), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule B (interest > $1,500), and either Form 8829 (actual home office) or the simplified method calculation reported on Schedule C. If you itemize (unlikely here) you would use Schedule A.

Detailed walk-through and calculations

1) Income summary (from what you gave)
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900
- Interest: Citi savings $1,560 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,755
Total gross income ≈ $93,005

2) Student loan interest
- The federal student‑loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 per return (subject to MAGI phaseouts). You can only deduct interest actually paid in 2023 (reported on Form 1098‑E or your records). Clarify whether the $4,400 is interest paid in 2023 or is an outstanding balance or payments made in earlier years — only 2023 payments count for 2023 filing.
- If you paid $2,500+ interest in 2023 and your MAGI is below the phaseout for MFJ, you can deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

3) Charitable gifts
- Your $2,000 cash gift to Northwest Harvest is deductible, but only if you itemize (Schedule A). A receipt is good documentation. Because the MFJ standard deduction for 2023 is $27,700, $2,000 of cash charity alone will not make itemizing beneficial unless you have other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses etc.).

4) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- Under current law (tax years 2018–2025) unreimbursed employee business expenses are not deductible for most employees. They do not reduce your federal taxable income.

5) Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year)
You are allowed a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employed web‑design business.

Two methods:

A. Simplified method
- IRS rate is $5 per sq ft (2023) up to 300 sq ft.
- Deduction = 100 sq ft × $5 = $500.
- Easy to apply; no Form 8829 required; just enter on Schedule C.

B. Actual expenses method
- Business use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4927%
- Rent portion = 14.4927% × $28,800 = $4,176. (You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. to increase this if you have those bills.)
- You must use Form 8829 to compute allowable home office deduction and keep supporting records.
- The actual method almost certainly gives a larger deduction here (≈ $4,176 vs $500).

Important constraints
- The home‑office deduction (actual method) is limited to net profits from that business (Schedule C). If the home‑office deduction would create or increase a net loss from the business beyond other business expenses, part of it may be suspended and carried forward — but with your numbers the allowable deduction (≈4,176) is less than your gross self‑employment income (8,900), so it will be allowed and will reduce your Schedule C net profit.
- A lower Schedule C profit also reduces self‑employment tax. You’ll compute self‑employment tax on Schedule SE and you can deduct half of that SE tax on Schedule 1 (adjustment to income).

Quick numeric comparison (approximate)
- If you use simplified: Schedule C net ≈ 8,900 − 500 = 8,400. Self‑employment tax and half‑SE deduction will be larger than under actual method.
- If you use actual: Schedule C net ≈ 8,900 − 4,176 = 4,724. This lowers both income and SE tax more, so actual is likely better for you given your high rent.

6) Itemize vs standard (recommendation)
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable pieces: $2,000 charity + (unknown) state tax withheld/property tax/mortgage interest. Unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other sizable itemized deductions, you will almost certainly be better off taking the $27,700 standard deduction. So take the standard deduction on Form 1040 unless you have other itemizable amounts you didn’t list.

7) Forms and where things go (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- W‑2 income reported on Form 1040 (W‑2 info).
- Schedule B — for interest income because your interest total ($1,755) is over $1,500.
- Schedule C — report the $8,900 self‑employment income and business expenses (including home office deduction — simplified or attach Form 8829 if using actual).
- Form 8829 — use if you choose the actual home‑office method (allocates rent, utilities, etc.).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax; half of this is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Schedule 1 — report adjustments: student loan interest (up to $2,500), deductible half of SE tax, etc.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look likely with info given).
- Keep supporting documents: receipts for charity, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), rent/utility statements, invoices for business expenses, 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC if you received any, and bank/1099‑INTs for interest.

8) California state return
- File California Form 540 (or 540NR if nonresident). California often conforms to many federal items but has differences — e.g., standard deduction amounts are smaller, and some federal adjustments may be treated differently. The home office deduction and student loan interest rules can differ in state treatment. If you want, I can outline CA-specific points once you confirm whether you own or rent (you said rent) and any state taxes withheld or paid.

9) Additional opportunities and notes
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, advertising, supplies, internet portion used for business, phone portion, education related to business, etc.). Those reduce net profit and SE tax.
- Consider retirement contributions (SEP IRA, Solo 401(k), or traditional IRA depending on eligibility) if you want to lower taxable income and save for retirement.
- You can use the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) on up to 20% of qualified business income from Schedule C if you meet the rules — for your size of business, it's likely available, but the exact amount depends on taxable income after deductions.
- Keep good records (bank statements, 1099s, receipts). For the home office actual method keep lease, rent payments, utilities bills, and a diagram showing the exclusive use area.

What I need from you (quick clarifying questions)
1. Was the $4,400 “student loan interest” amount paid in calendar year 2023, or is that the loan balance / payments from earlier years? Do you have a 2023 Form 1098‑E and how much interest does it show for 2023?
2. Do you have any mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state income tax payments that might push itemized deductions above $27,700?
3. Any other business expenses in 2023 (software subscriptions, home internet, advertising, supplies, mileage, equipment purchases)?
4. Do you want me to show a rough federal tax estimate comparing: (a) simplified home‑office + standard deduction vs (b) actual home‑office + standard deduction (I can show estimated AGI, taxable income, and how much tax/Self‑Employment tax changes)?

If you confirm the student‑loan timing and any other deductions (mortgage interest/SALT, other business expenses), I’ll run the quick numeric comparison and give the recommended method and exact forms/entries to use. Also happy to draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing or to help you prepare the return step‑by‑step.

