- Your self‑employment (web design) income must be reported on Schedule C. You can claim a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment work — either the simplified method ($5 × 100 sq ft = $500) or the regular/actual method (pro rata share of rent/utilities/etc.). If you use the actual method, you’ll generally use Form 8829.
- You will also owe (and must calculate) self‑employment tax on the net profit from the web design work and report it on Schedule SE; half of that SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040/Schedule 1.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension). If the $780 is business expense for your self‑employment work, it is deductible on Schedule C; if it’s unreimbursed expenses from your employer job, it is NOT deductible.
- You need a handful of federal forms (listed below) and likely California Form 540; see the checklist near the end.

Detailed breakdown and what to do

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $82,350 — reported on Form 1040 (W‑2 info).
- Self‑employment/web design: $8,900 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, keep them, but you report the income regardless of whether you got 1099s.
- Interest: $1,560 (Citi savings) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755 total interest — report as interest (Schedule B required if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500).
- Keep any 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B you received.

2) Student loan interest
- You paid $4,400 to FedLoan. The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040) and is limited to $2,500 (and phases out at higher MAGI).
- Based on the incomes you gave (total income ~ $93,000 before adjustments), you are under the usual MFJ phaseout threshold, so you should be able to claim the full $2,500. Keep Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer as proof.

3) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 cash to Northwest Harvest with a receipt — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for MFJ 2023 is $27,700, and your other likely itemizable amounts are small, itemizing is unlikely to beat the standard deduction. Keep the receipt for your records.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these $780 are business expenses for the self‑employment work (e.g., supplies, software, advertising), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed expenses incurred as an employee of Metro Consulting, they are NOT deductible in 2018–2025.

5) Home office treatment
- You said you have a 100 sq ft exclusive home office in a 690 sq ft home. Office percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (easy, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual method: take 14.49% of allowable home expenses. Example using the rent you gave:
     - Rent: $2,400/mo × 12 = $28,800/year. 14.49% of $28,800 ≈ $4,176.
     - Add the home share of utilities, internet (business portion), renter’s insurance, repairs apportioned to business, etc., to increase the deduction.
     - Use Form 8829 to compute the allowable business use of home deduction.
- Important limits:
  - Home‑office deduction for self‑employed individuals is taken on Schedule C.
  - The deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (excess may carry forward as an unused business loss under certain circumstances). If your net self‑employment profit is small, the home office deduction may be limited.
  - If you use the actual method, you must keep records (rent receipts, bills, square footage documentation).
- Given your self‑employment gross of $8,900, the actual method (rent share ≈ $4,176) is likely allowable because you still have positive net profit after other business expenses (unless the only business income is tiny or negative). But verify with your exact business expenses.

6) Self‑employment tax
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). Calculate using Schedule SE. The SE tax calculation uses 92.35% of net profit × 15.3% (approx effective rate ≈14.13% of net).
- You get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces your AGI).

7) Itemize vs standard
- Known itemizable amounts you gave: $2,000 charity.
- State & local taxes (income tax paid or property taxes) would add to Schedule A, but you didn’t list them. The SALT cap is $10,000.
- Because the 2023 standard deduction for MFJ is $27,700, and your known itemized items are small, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger. So you will likely take the standard deduction.

8) Forms you will need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax is reported here).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — report self‑employment income and business expenses.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the actual method for the home office).
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax calculation (and produces the half‑SE tax deduction reported on Schedule 1).
- Schedule B — if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (your interest total $1,755 → you will need Schedule B).
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) and 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / any 1099‑NEC for your records (you don’t attach all of them to the 1040, but you must report the amounts and keep the forms).
- California: Form 540 (California resident return) — CA has its own rules; many federal items carry over but some differences exist. Keep state instructions in mind.

9) Practical examples (two home‑office options)
