Summary (quick)
- Report total income: W‑2 $82,350 + self‑employment $8,900 + interest $1,560 + brokerage $195 = $93,005 gross.
- Student‑loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction but capped (see below).
- Home‑office deduction is allowed only for self‑employed work (Schedule C) and you can choose simplified or actual method — likely the actual rent share gives a bigger deduction here.
- Charitable gift ($2,000) is deductible only if you itemize; with the federal standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700 for 2023) you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have large other itemizable deductions.
- You must file Schedule C and Schedule SE because you have self‑employment income > $400; you’ll likely also need Schedule B (interest > $1,500) and Form 8995 (QBI) may apply.

What you can deduct (federal)
- Student‑loan interest: Up to $2,500 (maximum) as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line). You can only claim the allowed maximum (even though you paid $4,400). There are income phaseouts for married filing jointly (you get the full deduction if your MAGI is below the lower threshold; it phases out and is eliminated at the top threshold). You cannot claim this if you file Married Filing Separately.
- Charitable contribution ($2,000): Deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. You must keep the receipt — good supporting documentation.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780): Generally NOT deductible on your federal return for tax years 2018–2025 because miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. If those $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment work (not employee unreimbursed expenses), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office: If you use 100 sq ft exclusively and regularly for your web‑design business, you can claim a home‑office deduction on Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual expenses method: Deduct the business percentage of actual expenses. Your business percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.4928% (≈14.49%). Rent is $2,400/month → $28,800/year; 14.49% of that ≈ $4,175. You may also apportion utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. (You cannot take depreciation on a rented residence since you don’t own it, but you can deduct your share of rent and utilities.)
  - Important: home‑office expenses can’t exceed the net profit from the business. If allowable home‑office deductions exceed the self‑employment profit, the excess is carried forward (for self‑employed taxpayers).
- Self‑employment expenses: Ordinary and necessary business expenses for your web design work (software, hosting, business supplies, advertising, business portion of phone/internet, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Self‑employment tax: You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net SE earnings (Schedule SE). Half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- For 2023 the federal standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your only known itemizable amount is the $2,000 charitable gift. Unless you have substantial mortgage interest, property taxes, or other large deductible items, itemizing will produce a smaller deduction than the standard deduction. With the information provided, taking the standard deduction is almost certainly the better choice.
- If you do have mortgage interest or significant state/local taxes (up to the $10,000 SALT cap) or other deductible items, add them up and compare to $27,700 to decide.

Treatment of the home office (practical steps)
- You must use the space exclusively and regularly for business.
- Decide simplified vs actual method:
  - Simplified is easiest: $5 × sq ft.
  - Actual likely yields a bigger rent deduction for you (≈$4,175 of rent alone) based on the numbers you gave.
- Claim it on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). It reduces your net self‑employment income and therefore reduces both income tax and SE tax (subject to limitations noted above).
- Keep documentation: floor plan, photos, rent receipts, utility bills, receipts for any direct expenses, and a written allocation method.

Other important tax rules to note
- You must report the $8,900 self‑employment income on Schedule C, even if you didn’t receive a 1099. If you received 1099‑NEC(s), include them.
- If net self‑employment earnings > $400, pay SE tax (Schedule SE) and you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) if withholding isn’t sufficient.
- Interest reporting: total interest $1,560 + $195 = $1,755. Because interest/dividends exceed $1,500, you’ll file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% deduction of qualified business income (Form 8995). With your income level, you’re probably under the phase‑out threshold and so would generally be able to claim QBI — it’s calculated after Schedule C net profit.
- For the student loan interest deduction you should have Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid. For interest from Citi or E*TRADE you should have 1099‑INT/1099 forms.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for above‑the‑line deductions like half of SE tax and possibly student loan interest (depending on how IRS line numbering is presented; the adjustment flows to Form 1040)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses including home office portion
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction (if eligible)
- Form 1040‑ES (for estimated tax payments) — if you need to make quarterly payments
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), W‑2 from Metro Consulting, 1099‑INT(s), 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if any), and brokerage 1099s (if any)

California (state) filing
- File California Form 540 (resident return) and any required CA schedules. California has its own rules and does not always follow federal treatment for every deduction/adjustment — for example, CA may not conform to some federal above‑the‑line deductions or the QBI rules. Because state rules are different and can be technical, consider checking the California Franchise Tax Board guidance or using tax software or a tax advisor for CA specifics.

Records to gather before filing
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting
- 1099‑INTs (Citi), brokerage 1099 (E*TRADE)
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if any) and records of the $8,900 self‑employment receipts
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) or loan statements showing interest paid
- Donation receipt for Northwest Harvest $2,000
- Rent receipts, lease, utility bills, renter’s insurance (for home office calculation)
- Documentation for the $780 — clarify whether it’s self‑employment business expense or unreimbursed employee cost
- Records of any other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, state taxes paid)

Recommended next steps
1. Confirm whether the $780 is a business expense or an unreimbursed employee expense.
2. If it’s a business expense, gather receipts and decide whether to use the simplified or actual home‑office method (actual likely better here).
3. Gather the forms listed above and consider using tax software or a CPA — because you have self‑employment income, home office, and student loan interest, a preparer or good tax software will help ensure you claim the right adjustments and prepare Schedules C/SE correctly.
4. If you expect to owe taxes (because of SE tax), consider increasing W‑2 withholding or make estimated payments via Form 1040‑ES to avoid penalties.

