Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. Summary first, then specifics and the exact forms you’ll need.

Quick summary / bottom line
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) — your itemizable deductions (the $2,000 charity, etc.) are far below that unless you have substantial state/local tax or mortgage interest you didn’t list.
- For the web‑design income (self‑employment) you should claim a Schedule C and you can take a home‑office deduction. In your situation the actual‑expenses method (allocating a portion of rent to the office) will almost certainly give a bigger tax benefit than the simplified $5/sq ft method.
- You can deduct student loan interest, but only up to $2,500 (not the full $4,400 you paid), assuming you meet the income limits (your MAGI is well below the phaseout for MFJ).
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C (business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses), Schedule B (because your interest/dividends > $1,500), Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely), and supporting 1098‑E/1099 forms and CA Form 540 for state tax.

Detailed treatment and math (illustrative)
Given the numbers you provided:
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Web design (2 clients): $8,900 (self‑employment gross receipts)
- Citi savings interest: $1,560
- E*TRADE: $195
Total gross receipts shown here (approx): $93,005

Student loan interest
- You paid $4,400 but the above‑the‑line student‑loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 for 2023. You’ll claim up to $2,500 on your return (Form 1098‑E from FedLoan supports this). Phaseouts apply at much higher incomes (MFJ phaseout begins at $155k MAGI), so you should be eligible for the full $2,500.

Self‑employment (web design) and business expenses
- Eligible business expenses (reported on Schedule C) include ordinary and necessary costs of the web‑design work. You listed $780 of unreimbursed expenses — if those relate to your web‑design business, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If those are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (suspended through 2025).
- Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive use in a 690 sq ft apartment. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual expenses: allocate a share of your rent and other home expenses to the office. Your annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Office share = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%. Home‑office share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 0.1449 ≈ $4,174. (You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them.) Because your share of rent alone (~$4,174) is much larger than the $500 simplified amount, the actual method is much more beneficial here — provided you have no limitation from your net profit.
- Example using actual method (illustrative):
  - Schedule C gross receipts: $8,900
  - Expenses: $780 + $4,174 (home office share of rent) = $4,954
  - Net profit from business ≈ $3,946
  - Net earnings subject to SE tax = 92.35% × $3,946 ≈ $3,643
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × $3,643 ≈ $557
  - Deduction for one‑half SE tax (adjustment to income) ≈ $279
- Compare simplified method briefly: simplified gives higher net profit and a larger SE tax; in your case the actual method lowers both net profit and SE tax and therefore lowers your total taxable income. So use actual method and complete Form 8829 to calculate the allowable business‑use‑of‑home deduction.

Standard deduction vs itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your itemizable deductions listed: charitable $2,000 (you have a receipt). You didn’t list other large itemized items (mortgage interest, big state tax payments, etc.). Unless you have additional deductible items (large state income/property tax payments up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses above the AGI threshold, mortgage interest, etc.), the standard deduction will be larger.
- Practically: itemize only if your total Schedule A deductions exceed $27,700.

Forms and schedules you’ll use
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main)
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses, including the home‑office share
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculates SE tax; triggers the half‑SE deduction
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you elect actual expenses for the home office (not required for simplified method)
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half SE tax deduction are reported here (Schedule C net profit also flows to 1040)
  - Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required if total interest/dividends exceed $1,500 (your combined $1,560 + $195 > $1,500)
  - Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely)
- Supporting documents you'll need to have on hand / attach if applicable:
  - Form(s) 1099‑INT (Citi) and 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (E*TRADE) or consolidated 1099 from E*TRADE
  - Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) from FedLoan showing interest paid
  - 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC if your clients issued them for the web design work (income must be reported even if no 1099 was issued)
- State:
  - California Form 540 (CA state tax return) — follow CA rules (many federal adjustments flow differently to California; CA has its own treatment of student loan interest and itemized deductions, etc.)

Other practical points / tips
- Keep receipts and records: for the $2,000 charitable gift (receipt/acknowledgement), all business receipts (the $780 items), rent payments, and the 1098‑E.
- Make sure the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the self‑employment work. If they were employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and potentially additional income tax beyond what was withheld on your W‑2. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file, you should consider quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) for the current year to avoid underpayment penalties.
- If you sold securities, there may be 1099‑B and capital gains reporting; you only mentioned $195 — confirm what type of income that is.

Would you like me to:
- run the exact tax‑math for you (estimate AGI, taxable income, federal income tax and self‑employment tax) using the actual vs simplified home office methods; or
- prepare a checklist of documents and line‑by‑line where to enter each item on the forms; or
- draft an email you can send to FedLoan / FedLoan support to request Form 1098‑E (if you don’t have it)?

I can run the numeric tax estimate if you want — tell me whether the $780 expenses are for the web‑design business and confirm whether the $195 from E*TRADE is interest, dividends, or sale proceeds. Also tell me whether you received any 1099s and how much state tax (CA) was withheld on your W‑2 (if you want an estimate of refund/amount due).

