Below I assume the $4,400 was paid in 2023 and the $780 are business expenses for the web-design work; I’ll show alternatives where appropriate.

1) Summary of your 2023 gross income (based on the numbers you gave)
- W-2 Metro Consulting: $82,350
- Self-employment (web design): $8,900
- Interest from Citi savings: $1,560
- E*TRADE: $195
Total gross income: $93,005

2) Student loan interest
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (reported on Form 1040 Schedule 1), provided your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) is below the phase-out range. For MFJ in 2023 the phase-out begins at $145,000 and ends at $175,000 — based on the income you gave, you should be eligible for the full $2,500 (even if you actually paid $4,400).

3) Self-employment (web design) and the home office
- You must report the $8,900 self-employment income on Schedule C (Form 1040). Any ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the web design work are deductible on Schedule C (examples: software, hosting, supplies, portions of phone/internet used for business, business mileage, and the home-office deduction if you qualify).
- Home-office rules: to qualify the space must be used regularly and exclusively for your business and must be your principal place of business (or a place to meet clients, etc.). You said 100 sq ft exclusive out of a 690 sq ft home — that meets the exclusive-use test and gives a business-use percentage of about 14.49% (100 / 690).

Two ways to compute the home-office deduction
a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction. No Form 8829 required; you claim this on Schedule C.
b) Actual-expense method: multiply the business-use percentage (≈14.49%) by allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.). You indicated rent is $2,400/month → $28,800/year. 14.49% of $28,800 ≈ $4,172 of home-office deduction (you could include other home expenses proportionally if applicable). If you use the actual method you must file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.

Important limits
- Home-office deduction cannot exceed your net business income from that trade (Schedule C income after other business expenses). Excess home-office deduction may be carried forward if using the actual method. Simplified method is also limited by net business profit.
- Only self-employed taxpayers (Schedule C) can claim the home-office deduction. W-2 employees generally cannot.

4) Self-employment tax
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare) on 92.35% of net earnings at a 15.3% rate; you report SE tax on Schedule SE. Half of the SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

Example rough calculations (to show the effect of the home-office method). These are illustrative; I rounded to whole dollars.

Scenario A — simplified home-office ($500) and $780 business expenses (if those are business-related)
- Schedule C gross: 8,900
- Expenses: 500 (home) + 780 (other) = 1,280
- Net Schedule C profit: 7,620
- SE tax base = 7,620 * 0.9235 = 7,039 → SE tax ≈ $1,078 → half deductible ≈ $539
- Above-the-line deductions: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $539 = $3,039
- AGI ≈ 93,005 − 3,039 = 89,966
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2023) = $27,700 → Taxable income ≈ 62,266

Scenario B — actual home-office (rent portion ≈ $4,172) + $780 business expenses
- Schedule C expenses: 4,172 + 780 = 4,952
- Net Schedule C profit: 3,948
- SE tax base = 3,948 * 0.9235 = 3,645 → SE tax ≈ $558 → half deductible ≈ $279
- Above-the-line deductions: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $279 = $2,779
- AGI ≈ 93,005 − 2,779 = 90,226
- Standard deduction 27,700 → Taxable income ≈ 62,526

Takeaway from the examples: using the actual method gives a larger home-office deduction but because that reduces net Schedule C profit it also lowers SE tax and changes your above-the-line deduction only modestly. Both scenarios still leave taxable income far above what your $2,000 charitable gift would offset if you itemized.

5) Charitable contribution and itemizing vs standard deduction
- Your charitable gift of $2,000 is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. However, for 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700. Unless your total itemized deductions (state and local taxes up to $10,000 cap, mortgage interest, property taxes, charitable deductions, medical over 7.5% of AGI, etc.) exceed $27,700, you should take the standard deduction.
- Based on the info provided (only $2,000 in charity and no mortgage interest or big property tax numbers listed), it’s almost certainly better to take the standard deduction for 2023.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for a W‑2 employee) are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), except for a few specific categories (armed forces reservists, qualified performing artists, fee-basis state/local officials, certain disabled persons). If the $780 is employee unreimbursed, you cannot deduct it on your 2023 return.
- If the $780 are business expenses for your self-employment web design work, then they are deductible on Schedule C (as shown above).

7) What forms you will generally need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment and to report half SE tax if needed
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on net Schedule C profit
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual-expense method for the home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (probably not in your case)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received them from clients) — attach/keep records and report income regardless of receiving 1099s
- 1099‑INT (from Citi) and 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B (from E*TRADE) — report the interest/dividends (Schedule B if required)
- Keep Form 1098‑E from your student loan servicer (FedLoan) for your records — this shows interest paid

