User: I want to file my 2023 taxes correctly. I'm married filing jointly in San Diego CA. W-2 from Metro Consulting: $82,350. Additional income: $8,900 (web design, 2 clients), $1,560 from Citi savings, $195 from E*TRADE. Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive; I pay $2,400/month for 690 sq ft. Deductions: $4,400 student loan interest (FedLoan, 2018–2019); $2,000 to Northwest Harvest (food bank) with receipt; $780 unreimbursed expenses. Can you tell me what I can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and what forms I need?
Assistant: Summary answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly (2023 standard = $27,700) — based on the amounts you’ve given your itemizable deductions look smaller than the standard deduction.
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest (you paid $4,400, but the maximum deduction is $2,500 and it phases out at higher incomes — with the income figures you gave you’re well under the phase‑out for MFJ, so you should be able to take the full $2,500).
- Your web‑design income (self‑employment) is reported on Schedule C; you can take a home‑office deduction for the 100 sq ft exclusive office. You may use either the simplified method ($5/sq ft = $500) or the actual method (a share of rent/utilities/etc.). Based on the rent you reported, the actual method will likely give a larger deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for W‑2 work) are not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $780 is business expense tied to the web design work, claim it on Schedule C.
- You’ll need Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses), Schedule 1 (adjustments including student loan interest and the deduction for half of SE tax), and possibly Form 8995 (QBI) and Schedule A (only if you decide to itemize).

Details and numbers (walkthrough)
1) Income you reported (approx):
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900
- Interest: Citi savings $1,560 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,755
Total gross income ≈ $93,005 (assuming no other income or spouse income).

2) Student loan interest
- Maximum federal deduction is $2,500 per return (above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1).
- There is an income phaseout for 2023 for married filing jointly; with your ~ $93k gross you are well below the phaseout start, so you should be able to claim the full $2,500 (you paid $4,400, but you cannot deduct more than $2,500).
- You need Form 1098‑E (or lender statement) showing interest paid; keep that for your records.

3) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 cash gift to Northwest Harvest with receipt = deductible charitable contribution, but only on Schedule A (itemized).
- There is no above‑the‑line charity deduction for 2023 for most taxpayers, so if you take the standard deduction you do not also claim this $2,000 on Schedule A.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these $780 are personal employee unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.
- If they are ordinary & necessary expenses for your web design business (software, supplies, advertising, etc.), report them on Schedule C as business expenses.

5) Home‑office deduction (you said 100 sq ft exclusive; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/mo)
- Home‑office percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4928%.
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Actual method (rent portion only, ignoring utilities because you didn’t list them): 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,177. (If you paid utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., you can include the appropriate share as well — that will increase the deduction.)
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
- If the $780 are business expenses (not part of rent), add them to either method as ordinary business expenses on Schedule C.
- Example net self‑employment income (illustrative):
  - Using actual home‑office (rent portion ≈ $4,177) + $780 business expenses = total business deductions ≈ $4,957 → net SE income ≈ $8,900 − $4,957 = $3,943.
  - Using simplified + $780 = $500 + $780 = $1,280 → net SE income ≈ $8,900 − $1,280 = $7,620.
  - Conclusion: with the rent level you stated, the actual method will almost certainly produce a larger home‑office deduction (and lower taxable SE income) than the simplified method.
- How to claim:
  - Actual method: complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the deduction to Schedule C.
  - Simplified method: claim the $5/sq ft amount directly on Schedule C (and you do not file Form 8829).

6) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. You compute SE tax on Schedule SE (approx 15.3% of ~92.35% of net profit), and you can deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income from the web design business. With the relatively low net profit shown above you’d likely be able to take the QBI deduction; that deduction is computed on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A for more complex situations).

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your known potential itemizable deductions: $2,000 charitable + possible state & local taxes and mortgage interest (you didn’t list the latter). Without large state income or property taxes or mortgage interest, your itemized total is likely far below $27,700. So the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
- If you have sizable state income tax withholding, state estimated payments, property taxes, or mortgage interest that you didn’t list, add those up — if the total exceeds $27,700 (remember SALT cap $10,000), then consider itemizing. Otherwise use the standard deduction.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Attach your W‑2(s).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest deduction (adjustment) and to report one‑half SE tax adjustment.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use actual home‑office method. If you use simplified method, you do not file 8829; instead include the simplified home‑office amount on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax (and half deductible).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — if you claim the QBI deduction.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize.
- Schedule B — only if your taxable interest/dividend amounts exceed threshold (it’s required if taxable interest > $1,500).

9) California state return
- File California Form 540 (or the correct CA state form for 2023). California has its own rules will not always follow federal adjustments exactly (check student loan and SE items on CA return). Keep receipts and lender statements.

Records to keep
- 1098‑E (student loan interest), W‑2, any 1099‑INT/1099‑B, receipts for the $2,000 charity gift, receipts for business expenses ($780) and any utility/rent records that support the home‑office share, and records of rent paid (cancelled checks, bank statements) in case of audit.

Quick recommendations / next steps
- Likely take the standard deduction ($27,700) for 2023.
- Claim $2,500 student loan interest on Schedule 1 (verify with 1098‑E).
- Report web‑design income on Schedule C and use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) because your rent allocation (~$4,177) will almost certainly beat the $500 simplified method.
- If the $780 are business expenses, report them on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job they are not deductible.
- Complete Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax, and include half of SE as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Consider Form 8995 for the QBI deduction (likely beneficial).

If you want, I can:
- run a more detailed, line‑by‑line estimate of your federal tax (including SE tax and estimated refund/amount owed) using the numbers above, or
- prepare a checklist with every document you need to file, or
- show the exact lines on Form 1040/Schedules where each item goes.

