Summary answer (short):  
- Most likely take the 2023 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly = $27,700) — it will almost certainly be larger than the itemized deductions you listed.  
- You must report the web‑design income on Schedule C and pay SE tax (Schedule SE). Use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) rather than the $5/sqft simplified method — the actual allocation of rent gives a much larger deduction for your situation.  
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction up to $2,500 (not $4,400), subject to MAGI phaseouts (your income is likely low enough to claim the full $2,500).  
- Interest income totals $1,755 so you will generally report that on Schedule B (it’s over $1,500).  
- If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the web design work, they belong on Schedule C; if they are employee unreimbursed expenses they are not deductible for 2018–2025.  

Detailed explanation and steps

1) Income you must report
- W-2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040).  
- Self‑employment (web design) gross: $8,900 — report on Schedule C.  
  - If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are for that self‑employment activity, include them on Schedule C as business expenses.  
- Interest: $1,560 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755 — report on Form 1040; because total interest > $1,500, list it on Schedule B.  
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from the two clients should be included on Schedule C as well.

2) Home‑office treatment (self‑employed only)
- You said the office is exclusive use 100 sq ft of a 690 sq ft residence; rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year.  
- Actual method: business percentage = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%. Home‑office portion of rent = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176. This is reported on Schedule C (Form 8829 is used to compute the allowable actual home‑office deduction for self‑employed taxpayers).  
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500. In your case the actual method ($4,176) yields a much larger deduction.  
- Important limit: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed the net income from that business; however in your numbers the home‑office deduction (≈$4,176) is less than your gross self‑employment amount, so it should be allowable. Excess (if any) can be carried forward per Form 8829 rules.

3) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- After business expenses and home office, your Schedule C net profit will be reduced. That net profit is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute SE tax on Schedule SE; you may deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). This reduces AGI.

Quick approximate calculation (illustrative only)
- Self‑employment: 8,900 − 780 = 8,120 (business income before home office).  
- Less home office (actual) ≈ 4,176 → net profit ≈ 3,944.  
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 3,944 ≈ $558 (approx). Half SE tax deduction ≈ $279.  
- Student loan interest allowable (see next) up to $2,500 (an above‑the‑line deduction). Those two adjustments reduce AGI.

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (not $4,400). You can only deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income, and you must have paid that interest in 2023 (reported on Form 1098‑E from FedLoan). There is a MAGI phaseout for MFJ in 2023 (starts ~$145k and phases out by ~$175k) — your income as shown is well below that, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 cap. Keep the 1098‑E.

5) Charitable contributions
- $2,000 cash gift to Northwest Harvest (with receipt) is a deductible charitable contribution only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction for MFJ is $27,700, you will generally be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to $10k limit, unreimbursed medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.).

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction for 2023, Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.  
- Your listed itemizable items are small: $2,000 charitable + maybe state income tax withheld and property tax (you didn’t list amounts). Unless you paid several thousand dollars in state & local taxes or significant mortgage interest, itemizing will not exceed $27,700. Conclusion: you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — claim student loan interest (and half SE tax if applicable).  
- Schedule B — for interest income (because total interest > $1,500).  
- Schedule C — profit or loss from business (web design). Report gross receipts, business expenses (including the $780 if it’s business related), and the home‑office deduction (either directly or via Form 8829).  
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — to calculate the actual home‑office deduction (if you choose actual method). If you use the simplified method you don’t file 8829 (you just report the simplified home office deduction on Schedule C).  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the standard deduction).  
- Keep and attach/retain: W‑2, 1099‑INT(s), 1099‑NEC(s) if any, 1098‑E (student loan interest).  

8) State return (California)
- You also must file California state return. State rules differ from federal in some adjustments and treatment of certain deductions — confirm CA treatment of student loan interest and any other adjustments. Consider consulting a tax preparer for state specifics if you want to be sure about CA conformity.

9) Records to keep
- Receipts for the $2,000 donation (written acknowledgement if over $250), 1098‑E from FedLoan, bank statements and 1099‑INT forms, business receipts and invoices, proof of rent payments, photos and floor plan showing exclusive use of the 100 sq ft workspace, records for any utilities included in rent, and any 1099 forms.

10) Follow‑up questions I should know to be more precise
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses related to the web‑design work (i.e., business expenses) or are they employee expenses?  
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from those two web clients?  
- Do you pay mortgage interest or property tax (or other large deductible expenses) that you haven’t listed?  
- Is the $4,400 the amount reported on a 1098‑E for interest you paid in 2023 (or something else)?  

If you confirm those items I can:
- Run a tighter calculation showing estimated taxable income and approximate federal tax and self‑employment tax, and  
- Prepare a checklist of exact lines/forms to complete and which method to use for the home office (I already recommend actual/8829 for your figures).  

If you want, I can also draft the specific amounts to enter on Schedule C and 8829 (using actual numbers you confirm) or prepare a step‑by‑step filing checklist.

