Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer / bottom line
- You should take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700). Your likely itemized deductions (as stated) are far smaller than the standard deduction.
- You can (and should) report your web‑design income on Schedule C and claim a home‑office deduction for the 100 sq ft exclusive workspace. That reduces your self‑employment net income and self‑employment tax.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction, but it’s capped at $2,500 (not the $4,400 you paid). Because your income is well under the phaseout threshold, you should get the full $2,500.
- Required federal forms you will almost certainly need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use actual expenses for the home office), Schedule B (you have taxable interest > $1,500), and Schedule A only if you end up itemizing (unlikely). For California: Form 540 (or 540NR if not full‑year resident) and CA adjustments (Schedule CA).

Detailed explanation and calculations (estimates)
Income reported
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900 (report on Schedule C)
- Taxable interest/dividends: $1,560 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755 (because this total > $1,500 you should complete Schedule B)

Home‑office deduction (self‑employed)
You said 100 sq ft exclusive workspace in a 690 sq ft home:
- Business percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.49% (approx).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Rent allocable to business = 28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176.

You have two options to compute the home‑office deduction:
1) Actual method — allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) by the business percentage. In your case, rent alone gives about $4,176; if you pay utilities and can allocate a share, the deduction could be larger. Use Form 8829 to compute and report the deduction (Form 8829 is used when you use actual expenses).
2) Simplified method — $5.00 per square foot × 100 sq ft = $500 (max 300 sq ft). You would enter this amount directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

Given the rent you pay, the actual method almost certainly gives a bigger deduction (≈ $4,176 vs $500). Note: renters cannot depreciate the home (depreciation applies only to owners).

How the home office affects your federal tax computation
- Schedule C gross: $8,900
- Less business expenses: home‑office rent allocable ≈ $4,176 + your $780 unreimbursed expenses (if those $780 are business expenses for your web design work) = $4,956 total business expenses.
- Estimated net business income ≈ 8,900 − 4,956 = $3,944.

Self‑employment tax
- Self‑employment tax = 92.35% × net SE income × 15.3%.
- Using net ≈ $3,944: SE tax ≈ 0.9235 × 3,944 × 0.153 ≈ $557 (rounding).
- Half of that SE tax (~$279) is an above‑the‑line deduction and reduces your AGI.

Student‑loan interest
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (not the full $4,400 you paid).
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) and phases out for MFJ at higher MAGI (phaseout for 2023: $145,000–$175,000). Your estimated MAGI (with numbers above) is well below the phaseout, so you should be able to claim the full $2,500. (If your spouse has additional income, recheck MAGI.)

Itemize vs standard
- Your stated potential itemized deductions: $2,000 charitable cash gift (receipt) + any other itemizable items (you did not report mortgage interest, sizeable state/local taxes beyond withholdings, large medical expenses, etc.). That subtotal is ~ $2,000.
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction. Your charitable gift will not increase tax savings unless your total itemized deductions exceed $27,700.

Other items to consider
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if those $780 are expenses related to a W‑2 job) are not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension). If the $780 were business expenses for your self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C (as used above).
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI, Section 199A) deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income. With this small self‑employment profit, the QBI deduction will be small (roughly 20% × net business income after adjustments — maybe a few hundred dollars). The software or preparer will compute it precisely.
- You should consider estimated tax payments or increased withholding for 2024 if you expect similar self‑employment income (to cover income tax and self‑employment tax).

Which forms to file (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to claim student loan interest and other adjustments
- Schedule C — report web‑design income and business expenses (home‑office allocation goes here)
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you choose the actual method for business use of home (compute home‑office deduction); you do NOT file 8829 for the simplified method
- Schedule B — report interest and ordinary dividends (you have $1,755 of taxable interest, which is > $1,500)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (not recommended here)
- Form 1040-ES — for estimated tax payments in future tax years (if needed)

California (state)
- CA Form 540 (resident) — report the same income; CA has its own adjustments and no special home-office deduction separate from federal for renters (you generally start with federal AGI and adjust on the state form). You’ll complete CA’s Schedule CA (540) for itemized/adjustments as required.

Records to keep
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting
- 1099‑NEC or records for self‑employment income (if you didn’t get a 1099, keep invoices/bank records)
- Form 1099‑INT (Citi) and 1099‑DIV/1099‑INT from E*TRADE
- Student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E) or lender statement showing interest paid
- Charity receipt for $2,000 donation
- Rent receipts/lease to substantiate rent paid and square footage, plus receipts for utilities if you plan to allocate those
- Receipts/invoices for the $780 business expenses

Questions I have / recommended next steps
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your web design work or unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job? (If they’re employee expenses, they aren’t deductible; if they’re business expenses, include them on Schedule C.)
- Do you pay utilities, renter’s insurance, or other household expenses that should be allocated to the home office? If so, the actual method will likely increase the deduction further.
- Do both you and your spouse have income to report? If your spouse has separate income that raises MAGI, the student loan interest phaseout should be re‑checked.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft the Schedule C / rough tax worksheet with these numbers and two scenarios (actual home‑office vs simplified) so you can see the tax impact; or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer; or
- Help locate the specific lines on Form 1040 and the schedules for entering each item.

I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent — for large or unusual situations, or to confirm state specifics and phaseouts, consider running these numbers through tax software or consulting a tax preparer.

