1) Summary of treatment (by item)
- W-2 from Metro Consulting ($82,350)
  - Report on Form 1040 (W-2 wages go on Form 1040 wages line).

- Self‑employment/web design income ($8,900; 2 clients)
  - Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below).
  - Because you have self‑employment income > $400, you must file Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). Half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.

- Interest income ($1,560 from Citi savings + $195 from E*TRADE = $1,755)
  - Report as taxable interest on Form 1040.
  - Because total interest (and/or ordinary dividends) > $1,500, you will also file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; total residence 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year)
  - You qualify for a home office deduction only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business (you stated exclusive).
  - Two methods: simplified or actual.
    - Simplified: $5/sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
    - Actual (renter): allocate business percentage = 100/690 = 14.4927% of allowable home costs. On rent alone: 14.4927% × $28,800 = about $4,174 (approx. $4,173.91). If you pay utilities, renter’s insurance, or renter’s portion of repairs, a percentage of those likewise can be added.
  - The home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C (not 1040 directly) and cannot exceed the net profit from the business (excess home‑office deduction may be carried forward).
  - Choose the method that gives the larger allowed deduction (actual vs simplified). If actual method produces a deduction larger than your business profit, the excess is limited.

- Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
  - Clarify: if these $780 are expenses of your self‑employment/web design (e.g., software, supplies, travel for clients), enter them on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, those are not deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) — unless you are in a very specific category of employee (e.g., certain Armed Forces reservists, some educators, etc.). So probably they belong on Schedule C if they relate to the web design work.

- Student loan interest ($4,400 paid to FedLoan in 2018–2019? — you wrote 2018–2019)
  - If you paid $4,400 in 2023 interest, the federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 (above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1) and is subject to income phaseouts based on MAGI. If your 2023 MAGI (modified AGI) is below the phaseout range for MFJ you can take up to $2,500; above the range it phases out to $0. (We should check your MAGI; with stated income it’s likely you can take at least a portion or full $2,500, but I’ll show an estimate below.)
  - You should have Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid.

- Charitable contribution ($2,000 to Northwest Harvest, you have a receipt)
  - Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you cannot separately deduct it on the federal return (no universal charitable above‑the‑line deduction in 2023).
  - For cash gifts to qualified charities, keep the receipt.

2) Quick approximate math (estimates) — to show the impact and whether itemizing makes sense

Inputs
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Self-employment gross: $8,900
- Interest: $1,755 (Citi + E*TRADE)
- Student loan interest paid: $4,400 (you reported; limit applies)
- Rent: $2,400/mo = $28,800/yr
- Home office: 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 14.4927%
- Business other expenses: $780 (assumed business-related)

Schedule C (two home‑office options)

A) Using actual home office allocation (rent only)
- Home office (rent portion): ≈ $4,174
- Other business expenses: $780
- Total business expenses: ≈ $4,954
- Net self‑employment profit: 8,900 − 4,954 = $3,946 (approx)

B) Using simplified method
- Home office: $500
- Other business expenses: $780
- Total business expenses: $1,280
- Net self‑employment profit: 8,900 − 1,280 = $7,620

Note: simplified method yields higher net profit here (because rent allocation is large). But the simplified deduction gives only $500; actual gives larger home office deduction but reduces net profit more. Which is better depends on whether you prefer lower taxable net from self‑employment (actual) or higher net (simplified). However remember: lower net reduces self‑employment tax and also affects QBI calculation and taxable income. Evaluate both; you’ll pick whichever is more favorable overall.

Self‑employment tax (approx., using A actual example)
- Net profit ≈ $3,946 → SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (net × 0.9235) ≈ $558
- Half of SE tax above‑the‑line deduction ≈ $279

Federal adjustments and AGI (approx., using actual method A)
- Total income = 82,350 + 3,946 + 1,755 = 88,051
- Adjustments: half SE tax ≈ 279; student loan interest up to $2,500 (subject to phaseout)
- If you qualify for full $2,500:
  - AGI ≈ 88,051 − 279 − 2,500 = 85,272
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700
- Taxable income ≈ 85,272 − 27,700 = 57,572

If you used the simplified method (B), taxable income would be higher because net self‑employment income would be larger; but SE tax would also be larger and the home‑office deduction smaller. You can compute both and choose the method that yields the lower total tax.

Itemize vs standard
- Standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly, 2023): $27,700.
- Your only itemized items reported: charitable $2,000 (plus possibly state income tax / property tax you haven’t listed). Without substantial mortgage interest or large state/local taxes, your itemized total is far below $27,700, so you should take the standard deduction. (If you have significant state income tax or property tax payments not mentioned, recalculate; note SALT cap of $10,000.)

