What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses: If the $780 are business expenses for your web design work (supplies, software, advertising, etc.), those are deductible on Schedule C (not on Schedule A).
- Home-office for the self-employed: Allowed if you use the space exclusively and regularly for business. Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × square footage = 100 × $5 = $500 (no need to allocate rent/utility bills).
  - Regular (actual) method: prorate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Annual rent $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174 attributable to the home office (plus prorated share of utilities if you pay them).
  Note: you can choose whichever method gives the larger deduction; if you use the actual method you document rent/utility bills and use Form 8829 (see forms list).
- Self-employment tax: You must pay self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings (Schedule SE). The tax is about 15.3% on 92.35% of net profit. One-half of SE tax is an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: The federal student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction up to $2,500 per return (even though you paid $4,400, the deduction is capped at $2,500). This is subject to an income phaseout; with the income shown here, you will very likely be below the phaseout threshold and thus eligible for the full $2,500 — but I note that the $2,500 cap applies regardless.
- Charitable contributions: $2,000 cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct it on your federal return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If any of the $780 are unreimbursed employee business expenses (expenses related to a job where you received a W-2), these are not deductible on the federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act). Only self-employed business expenses are deductible.

Quick calculations (illustrative — use as guidance)
