Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and some state) forms you’ll use. I’ll also flag a couple of clarifying questions at the end that could change the recommendation.

Summary of the numbers you gave (2023)
- Metro Consulting W-2: $82,350
- Self-employment (web design, 2 clients): $8,900
- Citi savings interest: $1,560
- E*TRADE: $195
- Total rough gross income: $93,005

What you can deduct (federal)
1. Student loan interest (above-the-line adjustment)
- You reported $4,400 paid to FedLoan. The maximum student-loan-interest deduction is $2,500 per year (reported on Form 1040, Schedule 1, and reduces your AGI).
- There is an income phaseout, but with the income numbers you gave (AGI well below typical MFJ phaseout thresholds) you should be eligible for the full $2,500 unless there are large other adjustments that raise MAGI.

2. Self-employment deductions (Schedule C)
- You must report the $8,900 self-employment income on Schedule C and may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the web design work (software, hosting, office supplies, business mileage, etc.). If the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are business costs for the web design (not employee unreimbursed expenses for your W-2 job), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If the $780 were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return (suspended for 2018–2025).

3. Home office deduction (only for self-employed)
- Because you are self-employed for the web design income, you can claim a home office deduction if the 100 sq ft space is used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot (max 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  b) Actual-expense method: take the business-use percentage of actual home expenses. Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. You can allocate a portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allocable expenses. With rent of $2,400/month ($28,800/year), the rent portion alone would be about $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,175 — significantly larger than the $500 simplified-method amount. If you have utilities or other shared home expenses, the actual-method total could be higher.
- Note: Home office deductions are only for self-employment. You cannot claim a home office deduction as an employee.

4. Self-employment tax deduction
- You will owe self-employment tax on net profit from Schedule C (Social Security + Medicare). You can deduct one-half of self-employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

5. Charitable contributions
- You gave $2,000 to Northwest Harvest and have a receipt. That is an itemized charitable contribution (Schedule A).
- Charitable gifts are only useful if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction (see below), you do not separately deduct the $2,000 on Schedule A.

6. Miscellaneous
- Interest from Citi and payments from E*TRADE are taxable (report as interest/dividends). You should receive Form 1099-INT / 1099-B or similar.
- Keep Form 1098-E (or loan statements) to substantiate student loan interest.

Standard deduction vs. itemize (federal)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your known itemizable deductions:
  - Charitable cash: $2,000
  - (You gave no mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state/local tax amounts in your summary.)
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers (so that $780 is likely NOT an itemized deduction unless it is self-employment related and reported on Schedule C).
- Conclusion: With only $2,000 of charitable cash reported, you are very likely better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700). You would only itemize if you have other sizable itemized amounts (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income taxes, medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, or other deductible items) that weren’t listed.

Which home-office method to choose (recommendation)
- Based on the numbers you provided (100 sq ft of 690; rent $2,400/month), the actual-expense method that allocates a portion of your rent to the business will produce a substantially larger deduction (rough estimate: ~ $4,175 of rent allocated to business) versus the simplified $500.
- Because the home office deduction reduces your self-employment net profit (and therefore both income tax and self-employment tax), the actual method looks clearly more favorable here — assuming you are comfortable documenting rent and other household expenses and the space is exclusively used for business.
- If you prefer simplicity and minimal recordkeeping, use the simplified method ($500), but you will likely pay more tax overall.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and the deductible half of SE tax are reported here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web-design income and business expenses, including the home office deduction (if using the simplified method, enter the deduction directly on Schedule C)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute self-employment tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you choose the actual-expense method for the home office (do not use 8829 with the simplified method)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (probably you will not)
- Keep Forms 1099-NEC/1099-MISC (if received from clients), 1099-INT, 1099-B, and Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) for your records.

State (California)
- File California Form 540 (or the appropriate California resident return). California starts with federal AGI but has some differences; student loan interest rules and home-office treatment largely follow federal practice but check CA-specific rules or ask your tax preparer for any CA differences.
- CA may require Schedule CA (540) for adjustments; check instructions.

Quick approximate calculation (to show effect of home office method)
- Using the simplified home office ($500) and treating $780 as business expenses:
  - Net self-employment profit ≈ $8,900 - $780 - $500 = $7,620
  - Approx. self-employment tax ≈ $1,077; deductible half ≈ $539
  - Student loan interest deduction used = $2,500 (max)
  - Likely better take standard deduction ($27,700)
- Using actual home-office allocation of rent only (~14.49% of $28,800 = ~$4,175):
  - Net self-employment profit ≈ $8,900 - $780 - $4,175 = $3,945
  - SE tax ≈ $558; deductible half ≈ $279
  - This reduces taxable income significantly more than the simplified method. So actual method looks better in your case.

Recordkeeping you should have
- Form W-2 from Metro Consulting
- 1099-INT from Citi, 1099s from E*TRADE (interest/dividends)
- 1098-E or statements showing student loan interest paid
- Receipts/acknowledgement for the $2,000 charitable gift
- Rent receipts, lease agreement, and a floor plan showing the 100 sq ft exclusive business space; utility bills if using the actual method
- Records of business expenses (receipts, invoices) and any 1099s you received for the web design work

Other practical notes
- If you owe self-employment tax or additional income tax, you may need to pay estimated taxes or make an extra withholding to avoid penalties.
- You must report all income even if you didn’t receive a 1099.
- The home office space must be used regularly and exclusively for business — casual or shared use can disallow the deduction.
- If you’re unsure about the student loan interest phaseout or California-specific items, or if you have mortgage interest/property taxes not listed here, consider a preparer or CPA for final filing.

A couple of clarifying questions
1. Is the $780 of “unreimbursed expenses” for your web-design business (self-employment), or unreimbursed expenses related to your W-2 job? If it’s for the business, it’s deductible on Schedule C; if it’s employee unreimbursed expenses, it’s not deductible federally.
2. Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or significant state/local tax payments in 2023 (or other itemizable expenses)? If yes, itemizing might become advantageous.
3. Do you receive 1098-E from FedLoan for the student loan interest? (You should; keep it.)

If you want, I can:
- Estimate your final federal tax liability under the two home-office methods (I’ll use your answers to the clarifying questions if relevant), or
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect before you prepare or hand off your return.

