Short answer up front
- You can deduct: allowed business expenses for the web‑design work (including either the simplified or actual home‑office share of rent), the self‑employment tax adjustment (half of SE tax), up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest (subject to an income phaseout), and charitable gifts if you itemize.
- Whether to itemize: based on the numbers you gave, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly standard deduction for 2023 = $27,700). Your $2,000 charitable gift alone won’t beat the standard deduction unless you have significant other itemizable items (state tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses).
- Home office: report the web‑design income and expenses on Schedule C. You can use the simplified method ($5 × 100 sq ft = $500) or the actual method (your share of rent and other home expenses). With the numbers you gave, the actual method (rent proportion) looks substantially better.
- Forms you’ll need (federal): Form 1040, Schedule 1 (for adjustments such as student loan interest), Schedule C (business income/expenses), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 if you use the regular (actual) home‑office method, Schedule B (for interest if total interest > $1,500), and Schedule A only if you itemize. Also include your W‑2 and any 1099s/1099‑INT/1099‑B from E*TRADE.

Details, numbers, and reasoning

1) What is taxable income here (brief)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900
- Interest / investment: $1,560 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755
Gross income total ≈ $92, + about $ = $92,? (82,350 + 8,900 + 1,755 = $92, , 82,350+8,900=91,250 +1,755 = 93,005 — see sample calcs below)

2) Student loan interest
- Maximum federal above‑the‑line deduction is $2,500 (even though you paid $4,400). The deduction phases out at higher incomes; with the income levels you reported you should be under the phase‑out, so expect to take the full $2,500 (this reduces AGI and is reported on Schedule 1). Keep your FedLoan statements/1098‑E.

3) Charity
- $2,000 to Northwest Harvest is a qualified charitable gift and is deductible only if you itemize. You have the receipt — keep it (required for contributions of $250+). But because MFJ standard deduction is $27,700, you would only itemize if your total itemized deductions (charity + SALT up to $10k + mortgage interest + other eligible items) exceed $27,700.

4) Home office (most important for your situation)
- You meet the basic test: exclusive use of a specific area (100 sq ft) for business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5 × square footage (max 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft = $500 business deduction on Schedule C.
  b) Actual: allocate actual allowable home expenses by the business percentage (100 / 690 = 14.4928%). Your annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Rent share = 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,176. You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., which would increase the deduction (you didn’t list those amounts).
- With the figures you gave, actual method (just the rent share) gives ~ $4,176 vs. $500 simplified — the actual method appears much more favorable.
- How to claim:
  - If you use the simplified method: claim the $500 on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
  - If you use the actual method: complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer totals to Schedule C.
- Note: The home‑office deduction reduces self‑employment net profit (Schedule C) and therefore reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If those $780 are business expenses for the web‑design work (software, supplies, client expenses, ad costs, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal returns (suspended through 2025 by TCJA).

6) Self‑employment tax and related adjustment
- Net profit from the business (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (calculated on Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% of 92.35% of net profit.
- Half of the self‑employment tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).

7) Required federal forms (summary)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest deduction, etc.)
- Schedule B (if total interest/dividends > $1,500; your interest total $1,755 → Schedule B)
- Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment for web design)
- Schedule SE (self‑employment tax)
- Form 8829 (only if you choose the actual / regular home office method)
- Schedule A (only if you itemize; otherwise you won’t file Schedule A)
- Keep W‑2, 1099‑INTs, 1099‑B (if any stock sales), and any 1099‑NEC (if you received for contracting).

8) Quick apples‑to‑apples scenarios (rounded) — to show impact of home office method
Assumptions: $780 is business expense; student loan deduction limited to $2,500.

A. Actual home‑office method (rent share ≈ $4,176)
- Business expenses = $4,176 + $780 = $4,956
- Net self‑employment profit = $8,900 − $4,956 = $3,944
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $557 (half ≈ $279 deduction)
- Total income = 82,350 + 1,755 + 3,944 = $88,049
- Subtract half SE tax (~$279) and student loan interest $2,500 → AGI ≈ $85,270
- Standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $57,570

B. Simplified home‑office method ($500)
- Business expenses = $500 + $780 = $1,280
- Net self‑employment profit = $8,900 − $1,280 = $7,620
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $1,077 (half ≈ $539 deduction)
- Total income = 82,350 + 1,755 + 7,620 = $91,725
- Subtract half SE tax (~$539) and student loan interest $2,500 → AGI ≈ $88,687
- Standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $60,987

Conclusion from these scenarios: the actual method (using your rent share) reduces taxable income substantially more than the simplified method in your situation — so the actual method looks preferable. Because the home‑office deduction is part of business expenses on Schedule C, it will reduce your self‑employment tax and lower AGI.

