Short answer first
- For federal taxes you’ll almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) rather than itemizing based on the items you listed.
- You must report the web‑design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). That income is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax; you can deduct legitimate business expenses (including a home‑office share of your rent) on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line adjustment to income (Schedule 1) if you qualify.
- Charitable gifts are an itemized deduction (Schedule A) — they do not help if you take the standard deduction.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule B (because taxable interest > $1,500), Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), Form 8995 (if you claim QBI), and your California Form 540 (state return). I list details and numbers below.

Income summary (from what you gave)
- W‑2 wages (Metro Consulting): $82,350
- Self‑employment (web design): $8,900
- Taxable interest/dividends: $1,560 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755
- Rough gross taxable income before business expenses = $82,350 + $8,900 + $1,755 = $92, +? = $93,005

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment/business expenses: Direct, ordinary, necessary expenses for the web‑design work are deductible on Schedule C. That includes supplies, software, hosting, phone/use portion, advertising, etc.
  - The “$780 unreimbursed expenses” — important: are these related to your web‑design work (business expenses) or to your W‑2 job (employee unreimbursed expenses)? If they are business expenses for the web design, put them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on the federal return (TCJA suspension through 2025).
- Home‑office deduction (self‑employed): You may claim a deduction for the business use of your home if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business (you said 100 sq ft exclusive). Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 for 2023.
  - Actual (regular) method: allocate your actual home expenses by business use percentage. Your business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Business share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 0.1449 ≈ $4,174. (Plus a pro‑rata share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you pay these.)
  - Recommendation: Actual method looks better here (≈ $4,174 vs $500), so use Form 8829 (or the appropriate section of Schedule C) to compute and report it.
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings (calculated on Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: You paid $4,400 but the federal deduction is limited to $2,500 (subject to income limits). For MFJ in 2023 the phaseout starts well above your AGI, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 (assuming you are the person legally obligated on the loan and the loan is a qualifying student loan). This deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Charitable contribution: $2,000 to Northwest Harvest is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for MFJ 2023 is $27,700 and your likely other itemized deductions are small, itemizing will almost certainly be worse than taking the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: NOT deductible for most taxpayers (TCJA). If the $780 are business expenses for your web design business, include them on Schedule C instead.

Quick arithmetic example (estimate) — to illustrate how the home office choice and deductions change things
- If the $780 are business expenses and you use the actual home office method:
  - Schedule C gross: $8,900
  - Business expenses: Home‑office share of rent ≈ $4,174 + other business expenses $780 = $4,954
  - Net business profit ≈ $8,900 − $4,954 = $3,946
  - Self‑employment tax (approx): SE taxable ≈ 3,946 × 0.9235 ≈ $3,645; SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 3,645 ≈ $558. Half SE tax ≈ $279 (deductible on Schedule 1).
  - Student loan interest deduction = $2,500 (max allowed).
  - AGI effect: Your taxable income will be reduced by the Schedule C losses/expenses, the half‑SE deduction, and the student loan interest. This example shows the actual home‑office method gives you significantly more business deduction than the simplified $500.
Note: These are illustrative estimates; I didn’t compute your exact tax liability here.

Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- Your self‑employment net profit may qualify for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) — with small net profits the 20% deduction is small but available. It’s computed after business deductions but before the QBI deduction itself. You will likely be able to claim some QBI deduction.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest, half SE tax)
- Schedule B (if taxable interest and dividends > $1,500) — in your case total interest $1,755 → Schedule B required
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual method for the home office (optional if you use the simplified method)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction (likely Form 8995 for small taxpayers)
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (I expect you will not)

State (California) filing
- You’ll file California Form 540 (or the correct CA return). California follows many federal rules but does not conform to all federal provisions; some federal adjustments (and certain credits/deductions) are treated differently at the state level. I recommend checking CA Franchise Tax Board guidance or asking a tax preparer about CA treatment of the student loan interest deduction and any adjustments. (If you want, I can look up common CA differences for 2023.)

Records you should gather and keep
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting
