- 1099‑INT forms from Citi and E*TRADE (and 1099‑DIV if applicable)
- Form 1098‑E or statement showing student loan interest paid
- Charity receipt / acknowledgement for the $2,000 gift
- Proof of rent paid (leases, canceled checks, bank statements) and documentation of home square footage and home‑office square footage
- Receipts for the $780 and any other business expenses
- Records of any estimated tax payments or extra withholding

Practical recommendations / next steps
1. Confirm whether the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses (web design) or employee expenses. If business, put them on Schedule C. If employee, they’re not deductible federally.
2. Use the actual home‑office method (Form 8829) because the business share of rent (~$4,174) is much larger than the simplified $500.
3. Claim student loan interest of up to $2,500 on Schedule 1 (ensure you got 1098‑E or loan statement).
4. Take the standard deduction ($27,700) rather than itemizing — itemizing will almost certainly be smaller given the data you provided.
5. Be sure to file Schedule B (interest) and Schedule SE (self‑employment tax) and consider making estimated tax payments (or increase W‑2 withholding) to cover the tax and SE liability on the web‑design income.
6. Keep all receipts and documentation in case of questions or audit.

Anything else I can prepare for you?
- I can run a more precise, line‑by‑line estimate of your federal tax liability for 2023 if you want — I would need confirmation about whether the $780 are business expenses, whether the student loan is in your name, any other deductions or pre‑tax contributions (401(k), HSA), and whether you want me to use the actual or simplified home‑office method.
