Thanks — I can help. I’ll first summarize what’s deductible, then show a worked example (with clear assumptions), tell you whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, explain home-office treatment and the forms you’ll likely need, and finish with the questions I need to make this precise.

Short answers up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) — your known itemizable amounts (a $2,000 charitable gift) are well below that.
- Your self-employment (web design) activity can deduct business expenses (including a home-office share of rent) on Schedule C — that reduces your self-employment tax and income tax.
- Student-loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (up to $2,500 for 2023) but phases out at higher incomes (see below).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if they are employee expenses) are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If the $780 are business expenses tied to the web design work, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040 (federal), Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Form 8829 (if using actual home-office-expense method), Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for QBI deduction if applicable, Schedule A only if you itemize, and California Form 540 (state return). Also include whatever 1099s/1098/1099-INT/1099-B you received.

Assumptions I’ll use for the worked numbers below (please confirm/correct any that aren’t right)
- W-2 wages = $82,350 (you provided)
- Self-employment gross receipts from web design = $8,900
- Interest income = $1,560 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,755 (I treated the E*TRADE amount as taxable interest/dividend — if it’s a capital gain or qualified dividend the tax treatment differs)
- Student loan interest paid in 2023 = $4,400 (you reported); the deductible limit is $2,500 — phaseouts may apply if your MAGI is high (see below)
- Home: rent = $2,400/month for 690 sq ft => annual rent $28,800. Home office exclusive space = 100 sq ft.
- The $780 is assumed below to be business expenses for the web design work. If instead they are unreimbursed employee expenses, they are not deductible on your federal 2023 return.

Worked example (illustrative; I’ll show both simplified and actual home-office results)
1) Basic total income (before adjustments)
- W-2: 82,350
- Self-employment gross: 8,900
- Interest: 1,755
Total = 93,005

2) Home-office math
- Home-office percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%

A) Simplified home-office method
- Deduction = $5 × 100 sq ft = $500
- No Form 8829 needed. You claim this on Schedule C.

B) Actual-expense method (example using rent only; you can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc.)
- Rent portion deductible = $28,800 × 14.4927% ≈ $4,174
- If you include utilities/insurance/repairs proportionally, the deduction could be higher.
Note: For renters you cannot depreciate the home structure, but you do allocate rent and a share of utilities/insurance/repairs to the business.

3) Business expenses & net self-employment income (two scenarios)
Scenario 1 (use actual home-office rent allocation and include the $780 business expenses):
- Business expenses = $4,174 (home-office rent share) + $780 = $4,954
- Net self-employment profit = 8,900 − 4,954 = 3,946

Self-employment tax (approx)
- Taxable SE income for SE tax = 92.35% × net SE profit ≈ 0.9235 × 3,946 ≈ 3,644
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 3,644 ≈ $557
- Deductible half of SE tax (above-the-line) ≈ $279

Scenario 2 (use simplified home-office method)
- Business expenses = $500 + $780 = $1,280
- Net self-employment profit = 8,900 − 1,280 = 7,620
- SE tax calculation similarly larger; half SE tax deductible will be bigger.

4) Student loan interest adjustment
- Max deductible for 2023 = $2,500 (even if you paid $4,400). The deduction phases out by MAGI:
  - For married filing jointly the 2023 phaseout starts at MAGI ≈ $150,000 and phases out to $180,000 (if your MAGI is above the top of the phaseout, the deduction is disallowed). If your MAGI (after adjustments?) is below the phaseout threshold you can claim the full $2,500. If you want, I can estimate your MAGI precisely based on the numbers above and compute the allowed student-loan interest deduction.

5) Rough AGI and taxable-income example using Scenario 1 (actual home-office rent)
- Total income = 93,005
- Adjustments: student loan interest allowed (assume full $2,500 for this example) + half SE tax ~$279 = $2,779
- AGI ≈ 93,005 − 2,779 = 90,226
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2023) = $27,700
- Taxable income ≈ 90,226 − 27,700 = 62,526

Conclusion on itemizing vs standard
- With the known itemizable deduction you listed (charitable gift $2,000) you are far below the standard deduction for MFJ ($27,700). So you should almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- The home-office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it comes off your business income on Schedule C (so it still benefits you even if you take the standard deduction).

Other items you should consider / possible extra deductions
- QBI deduction: With self-employment net profit you may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995/8995-A) — up to 20% of qualified business income for many filers. With low net business profit the QBI amount will be modest but still worth checking.
- Retirement contributions (traditional IRA, SEP-IRA, Solo 401(k)) can reduce taxable income and SE tax exposure — worth considering for 2023 if you can still make contributions.
- Properly report any 1099-NEC (nonemployee compensation) you received from clients; if you didn’t receive one, you still must report the income.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — student loan interest is entered here (above-the-line)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report gross receipts ($8,900) and business expenses (home office, $780, etc.)
- Schedule SE — calculate self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — Use only if you choose the actual-expense method to compute business-use-of-home; not needed for the simplified method
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you want to claim the QBI deduction
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (doesn’t look beneficial here)
- Keep 1099-INTs (Citi, E*TRADE), 1099-NEC (if issued by clients), and any 1099-B/1099-DIV if you have investment transactions.

California state return
- File California Form 540 (or 540NR if nonresident). California generally starts from federal AGI but has its own adjustments and rules — check CA rules for student loan interest and other state-specific differences (I can check details if you want a CA-specific breakdown).

Records to keep
- Receipt for the $2,000 donation (you said you have it) — keep that documentation.
- Home-office documentation: floor plan showing exclusive use, calculations for square footage, rent/utility bills, and records of other household expenses allocated to the business.
- Records/receipts for the $780 business expenses.
- 1099s and bank statements showing interest income and payments.

Questions I need to finalize numbers and give exact guidance
1. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses related to your web-design business (self-employment) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a job? (If the latter, they are not deductible under current federal rules.)
2. Was the $4,400 student loan interest paid in 2023? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible for that tax year.)
3. Do you have 1099-NEC forms from the two clients, or did the clients not issue 1099s? (You must report gross receipts whether or not you got 1099s.)
4. Is the $195 from E*TRADE interest, dividends, or capital gains? (That affects reporting and forms.)
5. Any other deductible expenses (home utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) that can be allocated to the home office if you want to use the actual-expense method?

If you confirm the answers to those, I’ll:
- Re-do the numbers exactly (SE tax, AGI, allowable student loan interest after the MAGI phaseout if it applies).
- Show the exact federal forms and where each number goes (line numbers) and a quick CA note.
- Recommend whether to use simplified vs actual home-office method (I’ll compute both side-by-side).

I’m not a CPA or enrolled agent; this is general tax guidance. If your situation is complex or you want to minimize audit risk, consider a tax professional for final filing. Would you like me to recalc with Scenario 2 (simplified method) and/or proceed if you confirm the five questions above?

